  "worked_solution": "Since $f$ is a continuous function on $[2,7]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[2,7].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[2,7]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[2,3],$ we have $f(2)=3$ and $f(3)=1.$ Since $2\\in [1,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[3,6],$ we have $f(3)=1$ and $f(6)=4.$ Since $2\\in [1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[6,7],$ we have $f(6)=4$ and $f(7)=0.$ Since $2\\in [0,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $f(x)=2$ on the subintervals $[2,3],$ $[3,6]$ and $[6,7].$  Therefore, $f(x)=2$ has at least three solutions.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
